@*
    CaptchaWidget.razor
    Purpose: Self-hosted "prove you are human" control for every public write surface —
    the comment form, the rating step and the newsletter subscribe form.

    REQ-UI-056 / BRD-99 — challenge image + reload button + answer input, no third-party
    service and no third-party script.

    Two security properties this component is responsible for:
      1. The expected answer NEVER reaches the browser. ICaptchaService keeps it in a
         server-side cache; only an opaque challenge id and the rendered image cross the wire.
         The image itself is drawn by CaptchaSvgRenderer as anonymous <path> geometry — there is
         no <text> node, no character literal, no font reference and no id/class/transform that
         maps back to a glyph, so base64-decoding the data URI and parsing the SVG yields nothing
         but unlabelled strokes. Reading it back requires shape recognition, same as a human.
      2. The challenge is generated in OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender), which runs only in the
         interactive circuit — never during the static server prerender. A GET of the page
         therefore carries no challenge at all.

    REQ-UI-057 — ACCESSIBLE ALTERNATIVE (WCAG 2.1 AA 1.1.1). A purely visual challenge locks
    blind and low-vision visitors out of commenting, rating and subscribing entirely. This widget
    therefore offers a second, equally valid form of the same challenge:

      * A real <button> ("Can't see the image? Answer a question instead") sits in the natural tab
        order right under the control, so the alternative is reachable with the keyboard alone and
        is announced as a button — it is NOT a hover-only or pointer-only affordance.
      * In question mode the challenge is short prose ("What is seven plus two?"). That prose is
        the <label for> of the answer box, so every screen reader speaks the question when focus
        lands on the input, and it is ALSO pushed through a visually-hidden role="status"
        aria-live="polite" region so switching mode or asking for a new question is announced
        without moving focus.
      * The question is numeric on purpose. A multiple-choice question would print the answer in
        the page source — the very defect REQ-FN-049 had to fix for the image. Here the number the
        visitor works out appears nowhere in the DOM, nowhere in an aria-label, nowhere in a data
        attribute and nowhere in the circuit payload; CaptchaQuestionSet asserts that before the
        question is issued.
      * The alternative shares the visual challenge's lifecycle exactly — same server-side store,
        same 5-minute absolute expiry, same burn-before-compare single use — so the accessible
        route is not a cheaper one to replay.

    Public contract (consumed by PostView and the newsletter subscribe page):
        <CaptchaWidget @ref="captcha" data-testid="captcha-widget" />
        Task<bool> ValidateAsync()   -> true when the typed answer is correct
        void Reset()                 -> issues a fresh challenge in the CURRENT mode
        string ChallengeToken        -> the id to hand to a service that validates server-side
        string Answer                -> what the visitor typed, for the same purpose
        void ShowError(string)       -> surface a server-returned captcha failure inline

    IMPORTANT — a challenge is SINGLE USE. Call ValidateAsync() only when the receiving
    service does NOT validate the captcha itself. CommentSvc and RatingSvc DO validate, so
    those callers pass ChallengeToken + Answer straight through and never call ValidateAsync().

    Dependencies: BlogEngine.Services.ICaptchaService, TrBlazeUI Label/Input/Button, Lucide icons.
*@

@using System.Text
@inject BlogEngine.Services.ICaptchaService CaptchaService

<div class="flex flex-col gap-2 @Class" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">

    @*
        In question mode the label IS the question, so the input's accessible name carries it.
        The test hook sits on <Label> directly — TrBlazeUI 2.0.1 threw on an unmatched
        data-testid (TR-030) and forced a wrapper <span>; 2.0.2 splats it onto the <label>.
    *@
    <Label For="@inputElementId" data-testid="captcha-prompt">@PromptText</Label>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
        @if (!IsQuestionMode)
        {
            @if (challenge == null)
            {
                @* Reserves the image box so the row does not jump when the challenge arrives. *@
                <div class="h-16 w-[200px] rounded-md border border-border bg-muted"
                     aria-hidden="true"
                     data-testid="captcha-image-placeholder"></div>
            }
            else
            {
                <img src="@ImageDataUri"
                     width="190"
                     height="60"
                     alt="Verification image. Type the characters you see."
                     class="rounded-md border border-border"
                     data-testid="captcha-image" />
            }
        }

        <Button Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                Size="ButtonSize.Icon"
                Type="ButtonType.Button"
                OnClick="OnReloadClicked"
                AriaLabel="@ReloadLabel"
                title="@ReloadLabel"
                data-testid="captcha-reload">
            <LucideIcon Name="refresh-cw" Size="16" />
        </Button>

        <Input Id="@inputElementId"
               Value="@answer"
               ValueChanged="OnAnswerChanged"
               Placeholder="@AnswerPlaceholder"
               Class="@(IsQuestionMode ? "w-48" : "w-40")"
               AriaDescribedBy="@hintElementId"
               AriaInvalid="@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(errorMessage) ? (bool?)null : true)"
               autocomplete="off"
               autocapitalize="off"
               autocorrect="off"
               spellcheck="false"
               data-testid="captcha-answer" />
    </div>

    <span id="@hintElementId" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground" data-testid="captcha-hint">
        @CurrentHintText
    </span>

    @* The alternative is a real, focusable button in the natural tab order — never pointer-only. *@
    <div>
        <Button Variant="ButtonVariant.Link"
                Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                Type="ButtonType.Button"
                Class="h-auto px-0 text-xs"
                OnClick="OnModeToggleClicked"
                data-testid="captcha-mode-toggle">
            @ModeToggleText
        </Button>
    </div>

    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(errorMessage))
    {
        <span class="text-xs text-destructive" role="alert" aria-live="polite" data-testid="captcha-error">
            @errorMessage
        </span>
    }

    @* Announces a mode switch or a fresh question without stealing focus. *@
    <span class="sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-testid="captcha-status">
        @statusMessage
    </span>
</div>

@code {
    private readonly string inputElementId = $"captcha-answer-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";
    private readonly string hintElementId = $"captcha-hint-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    private BlogModels.CaptchaChallenge challenge;
    private string answer = string.Empty;
    private string errorMessage;
    private bool isQuestionMode;
    private string statusMessage = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// Label shown above the challenge, telling the visitor what to do.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string LabelText { get; set; } = "Type the characters shown";

    /// <summary>
    /// Reassurance text shown under the challenge and referenced by <c>aria-describedby</c>.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string HintText { get; set; } = "Generated and checked by this site — no third-party service.";

    /// <summary>
    /// Hint shown while the accessible question challenge is on screen. [REQ-UI-057]
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Deliberately contains no digit and no number word. A worked example would have been
    /// friendlier, but a constant number sitting next to the answer box makes "does the answer
    /// appear on the page?" impossible to check cleanly — and one day the constant would be the
    /// answer.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public string QuestionHintText { get; set; } = "Answer with digits or with the word — either is accepted.";

    /// <summary>
    /// Extra CSS classes applied to the widget's outer element.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Arbitrary attributes (<c>data-testid</c>, <c>aria-*</c>) forwarded to the outer element.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public Dictionary<string, object> AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The opaque id of the challenge currently on screen.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> Hand this to a service that validates the captcha itself
    /// (<c>CommentSvc</c>, <c>RatingSvc</c>) together with <see cref="Answer"/>. It carries no
    /// information about the expected answer.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> None.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    public string ChallengeToken => challenge?.ChallengeId;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the visitor typed into the answer box.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> Passed through to a validating service unchanged; the
    /// service normalises whitespace and case.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> None.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    public string Answer => answer;

    /// <summary>
    /// True when the visitor has typed something into the answer box.
    /// </summary>
    public bool HasAnswer => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(answer);

    /// <summary>
    /// True while the accessible text question is on screen instead of the image. [REQ-UI-057]
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> Exposed so a host page or a test can assert which form the
    /// visitor is being shown. It says nothing about the answer.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> None.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    public bool IsQuestionMode => isQuestionMode;

    /// <summary>
    /// The text of the <c>&lt;label for&gt;</c> above the answer box.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> In question mode this is the question itself, which makes the
    /// question the accessible name of the input — a screen reader speaks it the moment focus
    /// lands there, with no extra markup and no reliance on <c>aria-describedby</c> support.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> None.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    private string PromptText =>
        !isQuestionMode
            ? LabelText
            : string.IsNullOrEmpty(challenge?.QuestionText)
                ? "Loading the verification question…"
                : challenge.QuestionText;

    /// <summary>The accessible label and tooltip of the reload button in the current mode.</summary>
    private string ReloadLabel => isQuestionMode
        ? "Get a different verification question"
        : "Get a new verification image";

    /// <summary>The answer box placeholder in the current mode.</summary>
    private string AnswerPlaceholder => isQuestionMode ? "Your answer" : "Characters";

    /// <summary>The hint shown under the control in the current mode.</summary>
    private string CurrentHintText => isQuestionMode ? QuestionHintText : HintText;

    /// <summary>The label of the button that swaps between the two challenge forms.</summary>
    private string ModeToggleText => isQuestionMode
        ? "Show the verification image instead"
        : "Can't see the image? Answer a question instead";

    /// <summary>
    /// The current challenge image as a base64 <c>data:</c> URI.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> The SVG is encoded rather than inlined so the picture cannot
    /// be walked as DOM. It carries no character text either way — the renderer emits stroke
    /// geometry only — so the encoding is defence in depth, not the defence itself.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> None.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    private string ImageDataUri =>
        string.IsNullOrEmpty(challenge?.SvgMarkup)
            ? null
            : "data:image/svg+xml;base64," + Convert.ToBase64String(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(challenge.SvgMarkup));

    /// <summary>
    /// Issues the first challenge once the interactive circuit is live.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> Deliberately NOT <c>OnInitialized</c>: that also runs during
    /// the static prerender, which would put a challenge into the served HTML and burn a
    /// server-side cache entry for a visitor who may never connect.</para>
    /// <para><b>Flow:</b> first render only, generate, re-render.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> Adds one short-lived server-side cache entry.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="firstRender">True on the component's first render.</param>
    /// <returns>A task that completes when the challenge has been issued.</returns>
    protected override Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender || challenge != null)
            return Task.CompletedTask;

        IssueChallenge();
        StateHasChanged();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Checks the typed answer against the server-held expected value.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> Use this ONLY on a surface whose service does not validate
    /// the captcha itself — a challenge is single use and this call burns it. A wrong answer
    /// sets an inline error and issues a fresh challenge, because the old one is gone.</para>
    /// <para><b>Flow:</b> validate, on failure show the message and re-issue, on success clear.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> Consumes the current challenge; issues a new one.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <returns>True when the typed answer was correct.</returns>
    public Task<bool> ValidateAsync()
    {
        var result = CaptchaService.Validate(ChallengeToken, answer);
        if (result.IsFailure)
        {
            ShowError(result.ErrorMessage);
            return Task.FromResult(false);
        }

        errorMessage = null;
        answer = string.Empty;
        IssueChallenge();
        StateHasChanged();
        return Task.FromResult(true);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Issues a fresh challenge and clears the answer box and any error.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> Call this after a successful submission, or whenever the
    /// current challenge has been consumed elsewhere.</para>
    /// <para><b>Flow:</b> clear state, generate, re-render.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> Adds one short-lived server-side cache entry.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    public void Reset()
    {
        errorMessage = null;
        answer = string.Empty;
        IssueChallenge();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Shows a captcha failure returned by a validating service, and issues a fresh challenge.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> A service that validated and rejected the answer has already
    /// burned the challenge, so the visitor must be given a new image or every retry would fail
    /// with "expired".</para>
    /// <para><b>Flow:</b> keep the message, clear the answer, generate, re-render.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> Adds one short-lived server-side cache entry.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="message">The visitor-safe message to show under the control.</param>
    public void ShowError(string message)
    {
        errorMessage = !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(message)
            ? message
            : isQuestionMode
                ? "That was not the right answer. Please try the new verification question."
                : "The characters did not match. Please try the new image.";
        answer = string.Empty;
        IssueChallenge();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Replaces the on-screen challenge with a new one from the service.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> A failure to generate is swallowed into a visitor-safe
    /// message rather than taking the whole page down with it.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> Adds one short-lived server-side cache entry.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    private void IssueChallenge()
    {
        try
        {
            challenge = isQuestionMode
                ? CaptchaService.GenerateQuestion()
                : CaptchaService.Generate();

            Announce();
        }
        catch (BlogEngine.Common.CaptchaRateLimitedException rateLimited)
        {
            // REQ-NFR-024: the per-client issuance / failure caps refused this challenge. That is
            // an expected outcome, not a fault, and the visitor is told how long to wait rather
            // than being shown a blank control or a generic "could not be loaded".
            challenge = null;
            errorMessage = rateLimited.VisitorMessage;
            statusMessage = errorMessage;
        }
        catch
        {
            challenge = null;
            errorMessage = "The verification challenge could not be loaded. Please reload the page.";
            statusMessage = errorMessage;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Puts the current challenge into the visually-hidden live region.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> Swapping mode or asking for a new question changes the
    /// challenge without moving focus, so nothing would be spoken unless it is written into a
    /// <c>role="status" aria-live="polite"</c> region. The announcement repeats the question,
    /// never the answer. [REQ-UI-057]</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> None beyond the announced text.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    private void Announce()
    {
        statusMessage = isQuestionMode
            ? $"Verification question: {challenge?.QuestionText}"
            : "Verification image challenge. Type the characters you see, or use the button to answer a question instead.";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Handles the reload button.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> A visitor who cannot read the image — or cannot work out the
    /// question — gets a different one; the abandoned challenge simply expires.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> Issues a new challenge.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    private void OnReloadClicked()
    {
        Reset();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Swaps between the visual image challenge and the accessible question challenge.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>Business Logic:</b> WCAG 2.1 AA 1.1.1 — a visitor who cannot see the image must
    /// have an equally valid way through. The new challenge is a fresh one on the same
    /// server-side lifecycle, so nothing about switching makes the gate easier to defeat; the
    /// abandoned challenge is simply left to expire. [REQ-UI-057]</para>
    /// <para><b>Flow:</b> flip the mode, issue a challenge of the new kind, announce it.</para>
    /// <para><b>Side Effects:</b> Issues a new challenge and clears the answer box.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    private void OnModeToggleClicked()
    {
        isQuestionMode = !isQuestionMode;
        Reset();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Records what the visitor typed and clears a stale error.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="value">The new answer text.</param>
    private void OnAnswerChanged(string value)
    {
        answer = value ?? string.Empty;
        errorMessage = null;
    }
}
